WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.270
This walrus didn't get these dance moves on
his own.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.650
They came from Cab Calloway, a 1930s jazz
singer and band leader.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.200
Do you see it now?

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.830
Cab was a source of endless inspiration for
early animators, who transformed his dancing

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.390
into that walrus, and a ghost, and a very
moonwalky old man of the mountain.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:27.800
“The old man of the mountain!”

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.660
The way those moves got from real life to
cartoon was a breakthrough in technology and

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:33.660
method.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.820
It’s an idea that forever changed animation,
when an inventor took pictures that had just

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:48.930
started to move and made them dance.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:56.510
As…“riveting” as that was...early animation
had a problem: the first animated shorts didn’t

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.510
look right.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.290
Don’t focus on the drawing.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.670
Look at the motion.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.140
See how clunky his arm move is here?

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.320
And how his shoulder doesn’t move realistically?

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.400
Max Fleischer saw that problem too.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.750
This is him, the inventor, blowing bubbles
in some of the first films that revolutionized

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:14.750
animation.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.850
And this is the clown that did it.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.730
See how naturally Koko the clown moves compared
to the umbrella guy?

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.580
That’s where the invention called the rotoscope
comes in.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.020
You can understand it from the patent application.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.920
It was a way to film real movement to create
better animation.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.990
First, they filmed live action motion in the
wild — for Koko the clown, they filmed Max’s

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:46.350
brother, Dave Fleischer, dancing around in
a clown costume on Max’s roof.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.700
He was in front of a white sheet, for contrast.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:54.310
The sheet actually blew around so much that
once Dave almost fell off the roof.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.960
So, don’t try this at home.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.299
That film gave them individual frames of Koko
moving around, like in the patent.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:06.369
They used a projector, hooked up to a car
headlamp to amp up brightness, and it showed

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:09.520
each frame on a screen with tracing paper.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.019
Then they just played it back, frame by frame,
tracing what they needed.

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:18.900
It had the creativity of animation, but the
precision of live action.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.139
The results were astonishingly smooth, and
lots of people noticed.

00:02:22.139 --> 00:02:27.139
The New York Times said Koko, “The Inkwell
Man,” “leaps as a human being,” and

00:02:27.139 --> 00:02:30.769
it made sense — he was one.

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:32.849
Take Cab Calloway’s performance.

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:37.400
Now, animators didn’t have to guess what
subtle movements came in the middle.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.629
They had a filmed guide to every frame.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:52.630
Later, it helped out with Superman — using
photos and film to model Lois, like here.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:58.239
Gulliver’s Travels also had hyperreal movement
inspired by real motion.

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:01.849
When the patent expired, other animation studios
followed.

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:06.280
But Fleischer’s work was more than just
one invention.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.980
Now these cartoons and other ones at the time
are filled with tons of cringey stereotypes

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.049
that wouldn’t pass muster today.

00:03:13.049 --> 00:03:14.559
But the creativity?

00:03:14.559 --> 00:03:16.590
That, that is not dated at all.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.549
“Here we go!”

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:22.469
Fleischer studios invented the bouncing ball
song, where you can follow along with the

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:23.469
lyrics.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:24.999
Oh yes, there’s a patent.

00:03:24.999 --> 00:03:28.439
Max and Dave patented multiplane animation
as well.

00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:33.099
See how they could film the main character
moving and separately move the background

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:35.919
elements, like pictures and models?

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:38.379
This created depth and saved animators time.

00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:41.970
It enabled gorgeous motion like in this scene
from Superman.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:47.239
As it evolved, Fleischer animation mixed all
these technologies with skilled artistry and

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:48.239
improvisation.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.389
And that’s why rotoscoping is a versatile
tool still, whether it’s inspiring some

00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:58.409
of the animation in early video games or in
its logical extension in motion capture, where

00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:01.879
real movements are
given over to animators’ fancy.

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:04.719
But even that undersells their achievements
a little.

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:09.409
That Cab Calloway Walrus cartoon — Minnie
the Moocher — is a Betty Boop cartoon.

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:13.989
But it is a work of art filled with infinite
delights that tantali—...

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:14.989
Scratch that.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:19.509
It is straight up weird, in the best way possible.

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:25.110
Phones have lips, handkerchiefs talk, ghost
skeletons get drunk, tonsils scream — the

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.669
list goes on.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:32.389
When Cab Calloway saw himself turned into
a dancing walrus, he fell to the floor laughing.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:37.300
An invention made that work, but it was a
different type of genius that made Cab Calloway

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.610
fall to the floor.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.150
You can patent a device.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:42.780
But you can’t patent that.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:48.400
That’s it for this episode in this series
about big changes to movies that came from

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.810
outside of Hollywood.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:54.210
If there are any other animation examples
you find striking, let me know in the comments.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:59.330
I do want to take a chance though to underscore
just how far outside of Hollywood the Fleischers

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:05.490
were — in addition to their New York Studios,
they had one in Miami, Florida, and that is

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.790
where Gulliver’s Travels was actually made.

